;;; Ein — mixed value kinds in one hypothesis slot
;;;
;;; Two candidate `seat` facts whose second argument is the int `1` in one
;;; and the symbol `left` in the other, on a KB that is otherwise perfectly
;;; well-formed.
;;;
;;; What it pins. The commitment search orders its candidates by *content*
;;; (`Terms::cmp_fact_semantic`, called from `apriori::layer_1`):
;;; relation name first, then the arguments element-wise. Two candidates of
;;; the same relation whose slot *i* holds an int in one and a symbol in the
;;; other are therefore compared across value kinds, and `Value` is totally
;;; ordered by construction — `Int < Sym < Fact` across tags — so the pair
;;; sorts like any other and the run answers `Solution`, `?s = 1`.
;;;
;;; Why it needs an hrule. Blind hypothesis generation cannot reach it:
;;; `hypgen::candidate_objects` builds candidates out of `kb.names()`, which
;;; holds symbols only — so every blind candidate is all-symbols. An
;;; `hrule` has no such filter: its `:assert` args come from *bindings*, and a
;;; variable that matched an int arg carries the int through. Hence the shape
;;; below — one `hrule`, one variable, two facts that bind it to `1` and to
;;; `left`.
;;;
;;; `nested-fact-hypothesis.ein` is the other half of the same ordering: two
;;; candidates whose argument is a nested `Fact`, with no mixed slot types.
;;;
;;; Pinned by `corpus/corpus.toml` (the sweep runs it under every declared
;;; run, against a banked exit golden) and by `ein-infer/tests/interning.rs`,
;;; which uses it for a second thing: `Ann` is a name only the compiler ever
;;; sees.

(relation slotval T T)
(relation seat    T T)

;; The two bindings. `?v` matches the int 1 from one and the symbol `left`
;; from the other, and both reach `alive` as candidate `seat` facts.
(slotval x 1)
(slotval y left)

(hrule guess ()
  :match  (slotval ?k ?v)
  :assert (seat Ann ?v)
  :why    "guess that Ann sits at {?v}")

(query
  :goal   (seat Ann ?s)
  :hrules (guess)
)
